----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Test fixtures (VZ-FOUND-007, ADR-009)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

The fixture corpus contains NO third-party material and NO photograph taken by
anyone. Every fixture is synthesised by `cmd/fixturegen`: the pixels come from a
painter in internal/fixtures/raster.go driven by a fixed PRNG, and the metadata
— EXIF and GPS IFDs, PNG chunks, GIF blocks, the RIFF/VP8L bitstream, the
ISOBMFF box tree — is written byte by byte by that same package. Nothing is
downloaded at build time or at test time.

No licence therefore attaches to the corpus: its provenance is the script, which
is the reason ADR-009 chose a synthesised corpus over a collected one. The
corpus itself is not committed; fixtures/manifest.json is, and it records a
sha256, the dimensions, the expected decode outcome and this licence position
for every file.

Two of the twelve files could not be synthesised in pure Go, because AVIF needs
an AV1 encoder and WebM needs VP8/VP9/AV1, and no pure-Go encoder for those
exists:

  internal/fixtures/codec/avif-still.avif   libvips 8.18.2 (libheif 1.21.2/aom)
  internal/fixtures/codec/webm-short.webm   ffmpeg 8.1 (libvpx VP8)

Both were encoded from internal/fixtures/codec/source.png, which this generator
produced and which is committed beside them; a test asserts that the committed
source.png is still exactly what the generator emits, so the provenance chain is
checked rather than asserted. The exact commands are recorded in
fixtures/manifest.json under `codec_inputs`. Those two files are likewise
original synthetic material with no third-party rights in them.

The coordinates in the GPS fixture are the Royal Observatory, Greenwich. They
are nobody's home address, and they exist so that a later slice can prove GPS is
stripped from every derivative (ADR-006).

Real CC0 photographs, if any are ever added for visual QA, are listed with their
source URL and licence at that point (ADR-009). None is present today.
